{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192585 :question/math-academy-id "e-13995" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a+4b &=1 \\\\ 2a+b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$, \nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}(\\,}a+4b &=1{\\color{blue}\\,)}{\\color{blue}\\:\\times (-2)} \\\\ 2a+b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}-2a-8b} &{\\color{blue}{}=-2} \\\\ 2a+b &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-2a}-8b & =-2 \\\\ \\cancel{2a}+b & =-5 \\\\ (-8b+b) & =(-2-5) \\\\ -7b & =-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2},$ and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193241 :question/math-academy-id "q-210858" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - y &=1 \\\\ - 7x + 5y &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193242 :question/math-academy-id "q-210859" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t &=8 \\\\ s+5t &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $3,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $-3,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t &=8 \\\\ (\\,s+5t &=10\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t &=8 \\\\ -3s-15t &=-30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3s}+4t & =8 \\\\ -\\cancel{3s}-15t & =-30 \\\\ (4t-15t) & =(8-30) \\\\ -11t & =-22 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-3,$ and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508917 :question/math-academy-id "q-238035" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y &=36 \\\\ 2x+5y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $10,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-10,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y &=36 \\\\ (\\,2x+5y &=-4\\,)\\,\\times (-5)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y &=36 \\\\ -10x-25y &=20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10x}-3y & =36 \\\\ -\\cancel{10x}-25y & =20 \\\\ (-3y-25y) & =(36+20) \\\\ -28y & =56 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-5,$ and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509133 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-2s - 5t = -4; s - t = 7$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{-2s - 5t = -4; (s - t = 7)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2s - 5t &= -4 \\\\\n2s - 2t &= 14 \\\\\n(-5t - 2t) &= (-4 + 14) \\\\\n-7t &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $s$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509137 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{18s + 3t = 3; 3s - t = -1$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $18$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}-6}$, then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $-18$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $18$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{18s + 3t = 3; (3s - t = -1)\\cdot (-6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18s + 3t &= 3 \\\\\n-18s + 6t &= 6 \\\\\n(3t + 6t) &= (3 + 6) \\\\\n9t &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $s$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509162 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{4x - y = 7; -2x - 2y = -6$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{4x - y = 7; (-2x - 2y = -6)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - y &= 7 \\\\\n-4x - 4y &= -12 \\\\\n(-y - 4y) &= (7 - 12) \\\\\n-5y &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $x$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509164 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{s + 5t = 8; -6s - 3t = -11$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}6}$, then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(s + 5t = 8)\\cdot (6); -6s - 3t = -11\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6s + 30t &= 48 \\\\\n-6s - 3t &= -11 \\\\\n(30t - 3t) &= (48 - 11) \\\\\n27t &= 37\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $s$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509176 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-u + 7v = 0; 5u + 6v = -3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $5$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}5}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-5$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-u + 7v = 0)\\cdot (5); 5u + 6v = -3\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5u + 35v &= 0 \\\\\n5u + 6v &= -3 \\\\\n(35v + 6v) &= (0 - 3) \\\\\n41v &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509185 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-x + 6y = -10; 3x - 6y = -10$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$, then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-3$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-x + 6y = -10)\\cdot (3); 3x - 6y = -10\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 18y &= -30 \\\\\n3x - 6y &= -10 \\\\\n(18y - 6y) &= (-30 - 10) \\\\\n12y &= -40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $x$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509190 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{15u - 6v = -10; 3u - 8v = -12$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $15$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}-5}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-15$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $15$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{15u - 6v = -10; (3u - 8v = -12)\\cdot (-5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15u - 6v &= -10 \\\\\n-15u + 40v &= 60 \\\\\n(-6v + 40v) &= (-10 + 60) \\\\\n34v &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509195 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-2s + 10t = 5; -8s - 2t = 10$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-8$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-4}$, then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-8$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-2s + 10t = 5)\\cdot (-4); -8s - 2t = 10\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8s - 40t &= -20 \\\\\n-8s - 2t &= 10 \\\\\n(-40t - 2t) &= (-20 + 10) \\\\\n-42t &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $s$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-4}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509203 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{10x - y = 7; -2x - 2y = -6$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $10$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}5}$, then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-10$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{10x - y = 7; (-2x - 2y = -6)\\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x - y &= 7 \\\\\n-10x - 10y &= -30 \\\\\n(-y - 10y) &= (7 - 30) \\\\\n-11y &= -23\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $x$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509207 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{8u + 6v = 3; -4u + 5v = 8$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $-4$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{8u + 6v = 3; (-4u + 5v = 8)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8u + 6v &= 3 \\\\\n-8u + 10v &= 16 \\\\\n(6v + 10v) &= (3 + 16) \\\\\n16v &= 19\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509208 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{20u + 6v = 3; -4u + 5v = 8$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $20$, while in the second equation it is $-4$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}5}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-20$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $20$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{20u + 6v = 3; (-4u + 5v = 8)\\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20u + 6v &= 3 \\\\\n-20u + 25v &= 40 \\\\\n(6v + 25v) &= (3 + 40) \\\\\n31v &= 43\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509233 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{20u + 9v = 7; 4u - 8v = -12$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $20$, while in the second equation it is $4$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}-5}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-20$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $20$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{20u + 9v = 7; (4u - 8v = -12)\\cdot (-5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20u + 9v &= 7 \\\\\n-20u + 40v &= 60 \\\\\n(9v + 40v) &= (7 + 60) \\\\\n49v &= 67\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509238 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{3u + 6v = 3; -u + 5v = 8$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $u$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $u$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $3$, while in the second equation it is $-1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-3$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{3u + 6v = 3; (-u + 5v = 8)\\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3u + 6v &= 3 \\\\\n-3u + 15v &= 24 \\\\\n(6v + 15v) &= (3 + 24) \\\\\n21v &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$, and add the equations*."}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192586 :question/math-academy-id "e-13997" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=2 \\\\ x-2y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$, \nand add the equations.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2},$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\phantom{\\,(}3x+4y &=2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}(}\\,x-2y &=4\\,{\\color{blue})}\\:{\\color{blue}\\times (2)}\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}2x-4y} &{\\color{blue}{}=8}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{4y} & =8 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(2+8) \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$ and add the equations*."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193243 :question/math-academy-id "q-210947" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x + 10y &=3 \\\\ - 2x - y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193244 :question/math-academy-id "q-211000" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3w + 2z &=-1 \\\\ 7w + 10z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $z$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508249 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-8a + 4b = 5; -2a - 12b = 5$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-12$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-12$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-8a + 4b = 5)\\cdot (3); -2a - 12b = 5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-24a + 12b &= 15 \\\\\n-2a - 12b &= 5 \\\\\n(-24a - 2a) &= (15 + 5) \\\\\n-26a &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508268 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{5a - 4b = 6; 8a - 24b = -6$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-4$, while in the second equation it is $-24$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-6}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $24$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-24$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(5a - 4b = 6)\\cdot (-6); 8a - 24b = -6\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-30a + 24b &= -36 \\\\\n8a - 24b &= -6 \\\\\n(-30a + 8a) &= (-36 - 6) \\\\\n-22a &= -42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508298 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-8a + b = 5; -2a - 6b = 5$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}6}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-8a + b = 5)\\cdot (6); -2a - 6b = 5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-48a + 6b &= 30 \\\\\n-2a - 6b &= 5 \\\\\n(-48a - 2a) &= (30 + 5) \\\\\n-50a &= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508307 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\{a - 3b = -6; -7a - 15b = 9$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-3$, while in the second equation it is $-15$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-5}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $15$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-15$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(a - 3b = -6)\\cdot (-5); -7a - 15b = 9\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5a + 15b &= 30 \\\\\n-7a - 15b &= 9 \\\\\n(-5a - 7a) &= (30 + 9) \\\\\n-12a &= 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508321 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\{10a + 3b = -1; 9a + 18b = -5$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $3$, while in the second equation it is $18$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-6}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $-18$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $18$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(10a + 3b = -1)\\cdot (-6); 9a + 18b = -5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-60a - 18b &= 6 \\\\\n9a + 18b &= -5 \\\\\n(-60a + 9a) &= (6 - 5) \\\\\n-51a &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508322 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-3x - y = 5; -8x - 2y = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-3x - y = 5)\\cdot (-2); -8x - 2y = 3\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 2y &= -10 \\\\\n-8x - 2y &= 3 \\\\\n(6x - 8x) &= (-10 + 3) \\\\\n-2x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508327 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-3x - 2y = 5; -8x - 4y = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-4$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-3x - 2y = 5)\\cdot (-2); -8x - 4y = 3\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x + 4y &= -10 \\\\\n-8x - 4y &= 3 \\\\\n(6x - 8x) &= (-10 + 3) \\\\\n-2x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508333 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-5a - 15b = 8; -8a - 3b = 10$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-15$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}-5}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $15$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-15$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{-5a - 15b = 8; (-8a - 3b = 10)\\cdot (-5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5a - 15b &= 8 \\\\\n40a + 15b &= -50 \\\\\n(-5a + 40a) &= (8 - 50) \\\\\n35a &= -42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508342 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\{10a + 3b = -1; 9a + 9b = -5$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $3$, while in the second equation it is $9$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-3}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $-9$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $9$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(10a + 3b = -1)\\cdot (-3); 9a + 9b = -5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-30a - 9b &= 3 \\\\\n9a + 9b &= -5 \\\\\n(-30a + 9a) &= (3 - 5) \\\\\n-21a &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508345 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{-8a + 4b = 5; -2a - 24b = 5$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-24$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}6}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $24$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-24$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(-8a + 4b = 5)\\cdot (6); -2a - 24b = 5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-48a + 24b &= 30 \\\\\n-2a - 24b &= 5 \\\\\n(-48a - 2a) &= (30 + 5) \\\\\n-50a &= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508349 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\{a - b = -6; -7a - 3b = 9$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-3}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $3$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-3$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(a - b = -6)\\cdot (-3); -7a - 3b = 9\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3a + 3b &= 18 \\\\\n-7a - 3b &= 9 \\\\\n(-3a - 7a) &= (18 + 9) \\\\\n-10a &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508354 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{6x - y = 8; -6x + 2y = 6$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-1$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(6x - y = 8)\\cdot (2); -6x + 2y = 6\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - 2y &= 16 \\\\\n-6x + 2y &= 6 \\\\\n(12x - 6x) &= (16 + 6) \\\\\n6x &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508360 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\{10a + b = -1; 9a + 5b = -5$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $5$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-5}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $-5$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(10a + b = -1)\\cdot (-5); 9a + 5b = -5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-50a - 5b &= 5 \\\\\n9a + 5b &= -5 \\\\\n(-50a + 9a) &= (5 - 5) \\\\\n-41a &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-5}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508365 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\{a - 3b = -6; -7a - 6b = 9$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-3$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(a - 3b = -6)\\cdot (-2); -7a - 6b = 9\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2a + 6b &= 12 \\\\\n-7a - 6b &= 9 \\\\\n(-2a - 7a) &= (12 + 9) \\\\\n-9a &= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508370 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\{5a - 4b = 6; 8a - 12b = -6$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $b$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $b$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $-4$, while in the second equation it is $-12$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-3}$, then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-12$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(5a - 4b = 6)\\cdot (-3); 8a - 12b = -6\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-15a + 12b &= -18 \\\\\n8a - 12b &= -6 \\\\\n(-15a + 8a) &= (-18 - 6) \\\\\n-7a &= -24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $b$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}$, and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508913 :question/math-academy-id "q-238044" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+y &=8 \\\\ 8x-7y &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $y,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $-7.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $7,$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $7,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-7$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+y &=8\\,)\\,\\times (7) \\\\ 8x-7y &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-35x+7y &=56 \\\\ 8x-7y &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35x+\\cancel{7y} & =56 \\\\ 8x-\\cancel{7y} & =25 \\\\ (-35x+8x) & =(56+25) \\\\ -27x & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $7,$ and add the equations*."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508932 :question/math-academy-id "q-238051" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q &=11 \\\\ p+q &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q &=11 \\\\ (\\,p+q &=4\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q &=11 \\\\ -2p-2q &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3p+\\cancel{2q} & =11 \\\\ -2p-\\cancel{2q} & =-8 \\\\ (3p-2p) & =(11-8) \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $q,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-2,$ and add the equations*."}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192587 :question/math-academy-id "e-13999" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=8 \\\\ x-2y &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\phantom{\\,(}2x+3y &=8 \\\\ (\\,x-2y &=-3\\,)\\:\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=8 \\\\ -2x+4y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+3y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{-2x}+4y & =6 \\\\ (3y+4y) & =(8+6) \\\\ 7y & =14 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =-3 \\\\ x-2(2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,2).$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193226 :question/math-academy-id "q-18787" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y &=0 \\\\ 2x-3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $x.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y &=0 \\\\ (\\,2x-3y &=2\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y &=0 \\\\ -4x+6y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4x}-5y & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-4 \\\\ (-5y+6y) & =(0-4) \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=-4.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=-4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}4x-5y & =0 \\\\ 4x-5(-4) & =0 \\\\ 4x+20 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-20 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-5,\\,y=-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-5,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193236 :question/math-academy-id "q-208816" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + 3y &=5 \\\\ - x + 7y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508926 :question/math-academy-id "q-238082" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y &=21 \\\\ 2x+6y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the  first  equation is $6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y &=21 \\\\ (\\,2x+6y &=12\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y &=21 \\\\ -6x-18y &=-36\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{6x}+13y & =21 \\\\ -\\cancel{6x}-18y & =-36 \\\\ (13y-18y) & =(21-36) \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+13y & =21 \\\\ 6x+13(3) & =21 \\\\ 6x+39 & =21 \\\\ 6x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509050 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{-8x + 5y = 1; -2x - 7y = 25$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-8$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-4$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{-8x + 5y = 1; (-2x - 7y = 25) \\cdot (-4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 5y &= 1 \\\\\n8x + 28y &= -100 \\\\\n(5y + 28y) &= (1 - 100) \\\\\n33y &= -99 \\\\\ny &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = -3$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = -3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 7y &= 25 \\\\\n-2x - 7(-3) &= 25 \\\\\n-2x + 21 &= 25 \\\\\n-2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-3$, which can be expressed as $(-2,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509058 :question/problem "Find the solution $(a, b)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$\\{2a + 5b = 19; 14a - b = 25$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $a$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $14$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-7$, then the $a$-coefficient will become $-14$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $14$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(2a + 5b = 19) \\cdot (-7); 14a - b = 25\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-14a - 35b &= -133 \\\\\n14a - b &= 25 \\\\\n(-35b - b) &= (-133 + 25) \\\\\n-36b &= -108 \\\\\nb &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b = 3$. However, we still need to find $a$. To do this, we substitute $b = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a + 5b &= 19 \\\\\n2a + 5(3) &= 19 \\\\\n2a + 15 &= 19 \\\\\n2a &= 4 \\\\\na &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=2, \\,b=3$, which can be expressed as $(2,3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509066 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{12s - t = -10; 2s - 5t = 8$\n\nFind the solution $(s, t)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $s$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $12$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-6$, then the $s$-coefficient will become $-12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $12$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{12s - t = -10; (2s - 5t = 8) \\cdot (-6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12s - t &= -10 \\\\\n-12s + 30t &= -48 \\\\\n(-t + 30t) &= (-10 - 48) \\\\\n29t &= -58 \\\\\nt &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $t = -2$. However, we still need to find $s$. To do this, we substitute $t = -2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2s - 5t &= 8 \\\\\n2s - 5(-2) &= 8 \\\\\n2s + 10 &= 8 \\\\\n2s &= -2 \\\\\ns &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $s=-1, \\,t=-2$, which can be expressed as $(-1,-2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509068 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{2x + 3y = 1; 14x - 3y = -17$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $14$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-7$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $-14$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $14$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(2x + 3y = 1) \\cdot (-7); 14x - 3y = -17\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-14x - 21y &= -7 \\\\\n14x - 3y &= -17 \\\\\n(-21y - 3y) &= (-7 - 17) \\\\\n-24y &= -24 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 1$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3y &= 1 \\\\\n2x + 3(1) &= 1 \\\\\n2x + 3 &= 1 \\\\\n2x &= -2 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1, \\,y=1$, which can be expressed as $(-1,1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509081 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{9a + 4b = 16; -3a - 4b = 8$\n\nFind the solution $(a, b)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $a$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $9$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3$, then the $a$-coefficient will become $-9$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $9$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{9a + 4b = 16; (-3a - 4b = 8) \\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9a + 4b &= 16 \\\\\n-9a - 12b &= 24 \\\\\n(4b - 12b) &= (16 + 24) \\\\\n-8b &= 40 \\\\\nb &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b = -5$. However, we still need to find $a$. To do this, we substitute $b = -5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3a - 4b &= 8 \\\\\n-3a - 4(-5) &= 8 \\\\\n-3a + 20 &= 8 \\\\\n-3a &= -12 \\\\\na &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=4, \\,b=-5$, which can be expressed as $(4,-5)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509087 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{6s + 7t = -17; -s + 5t = 9$\n\nFind the solution $(s, t)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $s$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $-1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $6$, then the $s$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{6s + 7t = -17; (-s + 5t = 9) \\cdot (6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6s + 7t &= -17 \\\\\n-6s + 30t &= 54 \\\\\n(7t + 30t) &= (-17 + 54) \\\\\n37t &= 37 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $t = 1$. However, we still need to find $s$. To do this, we substitute $t = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-s + 5t &= 9 \\\\\n-s + 5(1) &= 9 \\\\\n-s + 5 &= 9 \\\\\ns &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $s=-4, \\,t=1$, which can be expressed as $(-4,1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509093 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{8x + y = -12; 2x - 7y = -32$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-4$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{8x + y = -12; (2x - 7y = -32) \\cdot (-4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + y &= -12 \\\\\n-8x + 28y &= 128 \\\\\n(y + 28y) &= (-12 + 128) \\\\\n29y &= 116 \\\\\ny &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 4$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 7y &= -32 \\\\\n2x - 7(4) &= -32 \\\\\n2x - 28 &= -32 \\\\\n2x &= -4 \\\\\nx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=4$, which can be expressed as $(-2,4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509100 :question/problem "Find the solution $(a, b)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$\\{-5a - 7b = -11; -a - 3b = 1$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $a$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $-5$, while in the second equation it is $-1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-5$, then the $a$-coefficient will become $5$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-5$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{-5a - 7b = -11; (-a - 3b = 1) \\cdot (-5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5a - 7b &= -11 \\\\\n5a + 15b &= -5 \\\\\n(-7b + 15b) &= (-11 - 5) \\\\\n8b &= -16 \\\\\nb &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b = -2$. However, we still need to find $a$. To do this, we substitute $b = -2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-a - 3b &= 1 \\\\\n-a - 3(-2) &= 1 \\\\\n-a + 6 &= 1 \\\\\na &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=5, \\,b=-2$, which can be expressed as $(5,-2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509103 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$\\{-12x - y = 39; -2x - 7y = 27$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $x$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-12$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-6$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-12$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{-12x - y = 39; (-2x - 7y = 27) \\cdot (-6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x - y &= 39 \\\\\n12x + 42y &= -162 \\\\\n(-y + 42y) &= (39 - 162) \\\\\n41y &= -123 \\\\\ny &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = -3$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = -3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 7y &= 27 \\\\\n-2x - 7(-3) &= 27 \\\\\n-2x + 21 &= 27 \\\\\n-2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3, \\,y=-3$, which can be expressed as $(-3,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509106 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{21u - 4v = -25; 3u + v = -2$\n\nFind the solution $(u, v)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $u$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $u$ in the first equation is $21$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-7$, then the $u$-coefficient will become $-21$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $21$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{21u - 4v = -25; (3u + v = -2) \\cdot (-7)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21u - 4v &= -25 \\\\\n-21u - 7v &= 14 \\\\\n(-4v - 7v) &= (-25 + 14) \\\\\n-11v &= -11 \\\\\nv &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $v = 1$. However, we still need to find $u$. To do this, we substitute $v = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3u + v &= -2 \\\\\n3u + (1) &= -2 \\\\\n3u + 1 &= -2 \\\\\n3u &= -3 \\\\\nu &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-1, \\,v=1$, which can be expressed as $(-1,1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509111 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{-4x + 5y = 10; x + 6y = -17$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $x$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-4$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $4$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{-4x + 5y = 10; (x + 6y = -17) \\cdot (4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 5y &= 10 \\\\\n4x + 24y &= -68 \\\\\n(5y + 24y) &= (10 - 68) \\\\\n29y &= -58 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = -2$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = -2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 6y &= -17 \\\\\nx + 6(-2) &= -17 \\\\\nx - 12 &= -17 \\\\\nx &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-5, \\,y=-2$, which can be expressed as $(-5,-2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509114 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{s + 3t = -14; 3s - t = -2$\n\nFind the solution $(s, t)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $s$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then the $s$-coefficient will become $-3$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{(s + 3t = -14) \\cdot (-3); 3s - t = -2\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3s - 9t &= 42 \\\\\n3s - t &= -2 \\\\\n(-9t - t) &= (42 - 2) \\\\\n-10t &= 40 \\\\\nt &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $t = -4$. However, we still need to find $s$. To do this, we substitute $t = -4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 3t &= -14 \\\\\ns + 3(-4) &= -14 \\\\\ns - 12 &= -14 \\\\\ns &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $s=-2, \\,t=-4$, which can be expressed as $(-2,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509115 :question/problem "Find the solution $(s, t)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$\\{8s + 6t = 22; 2s + t = 3$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $s$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-4$, then the $s$-coefficient will become $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{8s + 6t = 22; (2s + t = 3) \\cdot (-4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8s + 6t &= 22 \\\\\n-8s - 4t &= -12 \\\\\n(6t - 4t) &= (22 - 12) \\\\\n2t &= 10 \\\\\nt &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $t = 5$. However, we still need to find $s$. To do this, we substitute $t = 5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2s + t &= 3 \\\\\n2s + (5) &= 3 \\\\\n2s + 5 &= 3 \\\\\n2s &= -2 \\\\\ns &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $s=-1, \\,t=5$, which can be expressed as $(-1,5)$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192588 :question/math-academy-id "e-14002" :question/problem "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v &=3 \\\\ -3u+2v &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v &=3 \\\\ (\\,-3u+2v &=-5\\,)\\:\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\right. \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v &=3 \\\\ -9u+6v &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5u-\\cancel{6v} & =3 \\\\ -9u+\\cancel{6v} & =-15 \\\\ (5u-9u) & =(3-15) \\\\ -4u & =-12 \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+2v & =-5 \\\\ -3(3)+2v & =-5 \\\\ -9+2v & =-5 \\\\ 2v & =4 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u = 3, \\,v = 2,$ which can be expressed as $(3,2).$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193212 :question/math-academy-id "q-170145" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ 4x + y &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193285 :question/math-academy-id "q-238087" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y &=11 \\\\ 7x+4y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y.$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+2y &=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 7x+4y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-4y &=-22 \\\\ 7x+4y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\cancel{4y} & =-22 \\\\ 7x+\\cancel{4y} & =5 \\\\ (10x+7x) & =(-22+5) \\\\ 17x & =-17 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-1.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4y & =5 \\\\ 7(-1)+4y & =5 \\\\ -7+4y & =5 \\\\ 4y & =12 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508927 :question/math-academy-id "q-170159" :question/problem "Find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q &=11 \\\\ 5p+4q &=19\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $q.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $q$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $q$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3p+2q &=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 5p+4q &=19\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p-4q &=-22 \\\\ 5p+4q &=19\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-6p-\\cancel{4q} & =-22 \\\\ 5p+\\cancel{4q} & =19 \\\\ (-6p+5p) & =(-22+19) \\\\ -p & =-3 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $p=3.$ However, we still need to find $q.$ To do this, we substitute $p=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+2q & =11 \\\\ 3(3)+2q & =11 \\\\ 9+2q & =11 \\\\ 2q & =2 \\\\ q & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509267 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+y&=16\\\\-7x-6y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(5x+y=16\\right)\\cdot (6); -7x-6y=-4\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30x+6y&=96 \\\\\n-7x-6y&=-4 \\\\\n(30x-7x)&=(96-4) \\\\\n23x&=92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+y&=16 \\\\\n5(4)+y&=16 \\\\\n20+y&=16 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509270 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+6y&=-17\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ x+6y=-17; \\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+6y&=-17 \\\\\n21x-6y&=39 \\\\\n(x+21x)&=(-17+39) \\\\\n22x&=22 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509271 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+14y&=-36\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $14$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-14$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $14$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+14y=-36; \\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (7)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+14y&=-36 \\\\\n-49x-14y&=-140 \\\\\n(5x-49x)&=(-36-140) \\\\\n-44x&=-176 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509278 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y&=-7\\\\x-6y&=22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(-x+y=-7\\right)\\cdot (6); x-6y=22\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x+6y&=-42 \\\\\nx-6y&=22 \\\\\n(-6x+x)&=(-42+22) \\\\\n-5x&=-20 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+y&=-7 \\\\\n-1(4)+y&=-7 \\\\\n-4+y&=-7 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509288 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y&=8\\\\3x+y&=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-4$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ -x+4y=8; \\left(3x+y=15\\right)\\cdot (-4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+4y&=8 \\\\\n-12x-4y&=-60 \\\\\n(-x-12x)&=(8-60) \\\\\n-13x&=-52 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x+y&=15 \\\\\n3(4)+y&=15 \\\\\n12+y&=15 \\\\\ny&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = 3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509302 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y&=5\\\\7x-6y&=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(x-2y=5\\right)\\cdot (-3); 7x-6y=11\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x+6y&=-15 \\\\\n7x-6y&=11 \\\\\n(-3x+7x)&=(-15+11) \\\\\n4x&=-4 \\\\\nx&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx-2y&=5 \\\\\n1(-1)-2y&=5 \\\\\n-1-2y&=5 \\\\\n-2y&=6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-1,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509308 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+12y&=-35\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $12$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $12$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ x+12y=-35; \\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+12y&=-35 \\\\\n42x-12y&=78 \\\\\n(x+42x)&=(-35+78) \\\\\n43x&=43 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509310 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-2y&=6\\\\3x+10y&=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $10$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $5$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-10$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(-5x-2y=6\\right)\\cdot (5); 3x+10y=14\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-25x-10y&=30 \\\\\n3x+10y&=14 \\\\\n(-25x+3x)&=(30+14) \\\\\n-22x&=44 \\\\\nx&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -2$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x-2y&=6 \\\\\n-5(-2)-2y&=6 \\\\\n10-2y&=6 \\\\\n-2y&=-4 \\\\\ny&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -2, \\,y = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-2,2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509318 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+4y&=-1\\\\x+2y&=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+4y=-1; \\left(x+2y=1\\right)\\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+4y&=-1 \\\\\n-2x-4y&=-2 \\\\\n(5x-2x)&=(-1-2) \\\\\n3x&=-3 \\\\\nx&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+2y&=1 \\\\\n1(-1)+2y&=1 \\\\\n-1+2y&=1 \\\\\n2y&=2 \\\\\ny&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\,y = 1\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-1,1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509328 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y&=-12\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $4$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+8y=-12; \\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+8y&=-12 \\\\\n-28x-8y&=-80 \\\\\n(5x-28x)&=(-12-80) \\\\\n-23x&=-92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509333 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y&=13\\\\5x-21y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-3$, while in the second equation it is $-21$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $21$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-21$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(-2x-3y=13\\right)\\cdot (-7); 5x-21y=-4\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14x+21y&=-91 \\\\\n5x-21y&=-4 \\\\\n(14x+5x)&=(-91-4) \\\\\n19x&=-95 \\\\\nx&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -5$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x-3y&=13 \\\\\n-2(-5)-3y&=13 \\\\\n10-3y&=13 \\\\\n-3y&=3 \\\\\ny&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -5, \\,y = -1\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-5,-1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509339 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+6y&=-33\\\\2x-3y&=21\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $2$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ -x+6y=-33; \\left(2x-3y=21\\right)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+6y&=-33 \\\\\n4x-6y&=42 \\\\\n(-x+4x)&=(-33+42) \\\\\n3x&=9 \\\\\nx&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 3$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x-3y&=21 \\\\\n2(3)-3y&=21 \\\\\n6-3y&=21 \\\\\n-3y&=15 \\\\\ny&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 3, \\,y = -5\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,-5)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509344 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+15y&=32\\\\-7x-3y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $15$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $5$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-15$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $15$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 2x+15y=32; \\left(-7x-3y=-13\\right)\\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+15y&=32 \\\\\n-35x-15y&=-65 \\\\\n(2x-35x)&=(32-65) \\\\\n-33x&=-33 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-7-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-3y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,2)$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: One Transformation"}
